#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135015 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to evaluate the expression $\\sqrt x + 3$ for the value $x=9.$ This type of expression is called a **radical expression** because it contains the radical symbol $\\sqrt{\\phantom 0}.$ Remember that $\\sqrt x$ means \"the positive square root of the number $x.$\" \n\nTo evaluate this expression at the given value, we replace $x$ with $9$ in the given expression and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9}+3 & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{3}^{2}}+3 & = \\\\ 3+3 & = \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Important:** You may have heard somewhere that $\\sqrt{9} = \\pm 3,$ because $(-3)^2=9$ and $(3)^2 = 9.$ However, when we write down $\\sqrt{9},$ we *always* mean \"the positive square root of $9$\".\n\nIf we want the negative square root of $9$, we write $-\\sqrt{9} = -3.$ Sometimes, we want both, in which case we would write $\\pm\\sqrt{9}=\\pm 3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4457c20d-88c9-5028-8390-390579fa7229" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1610 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
